const THE_TEST = "THE TEST (brief ADDENDUM 14, the owner 2026-09-12; re-worded by the Fable sitting, ADDENDUM 18 and " + CONTRA + " section V): A FACE IS LAWFUL UNLESS IT CONTRADICTS THE RECORD. SILENCE IN THE RECORD IS PERMISSION. 'The card does not license it' names no fault. What you write is a PLOT HOOK and a DESCRIPTION a game master reads to be immersed and changes at will, never a receipt. THE FOUR FLOORS ARE THE WHOLE OF THE LAW. (1) NO SELF-CONTRADICTION ON THE PAGE — the unit is the whole town record; FOUR ROSTERS ARE CLOSED TO THE KEYS (the institution roster over the live roster, the force buckets, the faction list, the faith entries): a body, building, record-keeper, force or faith-house they do not carry may not be asserted — and NEITHER MAY A BODY BE INFERRED INTO A KEY'S SILENCE where a required roster row or a machine sentence on the same tab denies it (the sitting found this, not the predicted floor-2 failure, to be where the re-cut prose actually fails: a muster ROLL on a town whose only roll-keeper is a militia it does not have; a garrison 'quartered among houses' where the only garrison row is a Barracks; 'nobody is set up to respond' where a Town watch and a Town hall are required at town). A face is lawful iff no field in its tab's SAME-PAGE READ SET, at any value the keys admit, denies it; the card lists that set. (2) MAGNITUDE AND TENSE — a magnitude (count, share, size, distance, duration, frequency, proportion, age) only in the band word the read hands you, never as a digit or a word ('a handful', 'most nights' are magnitudes); no date, no rate ('every spring', 'more often than not'); no event the record did not run; and OVER A LIVE FIELD no elapsed course (has stood, still, no longer, since, again, thinner than it was) — BUT OVER THE POOL KEY'S OWN READS (a face is drawn only while its key holds, so it cannot outlive the state it describes) AND OVER A FROZEN FIELD (a read with zero writers after generation; the card prints the census) the perfect and the durative are LICENSED: 'nobody has been asked to stand on the works' is lawful where the works and the force are frozen reads. The simple habitual present is lawful everywhere. (3) SCOPE — no NAMED character's fate; nothing predicated of a deity (the followers act, the god does not); world-only, sub-century, setting-agnostic (eleven culture profiles ship: no thatch, churchyard or market green unless the profile is one that has them); an UNNAMED person may act, hold a key, refuse, be resented — never in the singular office the tier names as an NPC (the Guard Captain, the Mayor, the High Priest, the Moneylender under debt, the Healer under plague: those people are on the next tab with a personality and a secret). (4) THE ENGINE'S MODEL — where the engine positively models a thing, prose may not deny it: no material decay clock and no permanence (calamity, razing and the purse remove fabric); plagued is monsters; one military purse (the wall's keeping and the soldiers' wages together) and the gates never split direction; a covert fact (a hidden faction, an unexposed corrupt officer, a birth capture) never on a player face. THE INSTRUMENT: " + CONTRA + " — its section V governs where it and an earlier row disagree; read your desk's rows, the header list of WHAT IS NO LONGER A FINDING, and section V. "

const VOICE = "THE VOICE (brief ADDENDUM 18 ruling 13 as the owner qualified it: THE MASTER ARCHIVER'S HAND). One central archiver — professional, unbiased as far as a person can be, and still human — compiles the dossier from what the clerks of the various halls send in. EVERY FACE HAS A SOURCE WITH A STAKE, and the ARCHIVER REPORTS IT: the clerks never speak in the first person; they are reported in attributed speech, and the attribution carries the archiver's own stance toward the source — 'the hall would like it noted that', 'the crown's assessor holds that', 'the guilds were public in stating that', 'the tavern's view is that', 'it is said', 'it is not recorded that'. A FACT THE ENGINE HOLDS STANDS BARE (ruling 40, the owner's: 'the survey should not refer to itself'): the spine and any face that states a recorded fact says it plainly in the archiver's own hand — 'The walls are kept and no soldiers of the town's own stand behind them' — NEVER 'the survey finds', 'so far as the survey can find', 'the record has', 'entered as', 'set down here', 'this office', 'in the survey's time here' (the E2 ratchet refuses these on a player face); EVERY ACCOUNT NAMES ITS SOURCE (an opinion or report can be partial or wrong; a recorded fact cannot); the public is the human witness of what everyone saw; a spine may be carried by the public or a source instead of the bare fact, and a pool's three spines take three different SHAPES (ruling 29) — or a SPECIFIC record this town's roster keeps, by name (a muster roll only where a militia keeps one; a toll book only where a gate is held; the parish register only where a church stands; the accounts only where a hall or treasury keeps them — F1-24); never 'the books' as a generic; a bare assertion is the archiver claiming knowledge of its own, and the selector refuses it; ONE FRAME MAY CARRY TWO CLAUSES ('by the tavern's account the watch is paid late, and by the survey's the purse that pays it runs short'), and the selector vetoes the same attribution verb three times running; the archiver's own hand appears in every bare statement of a recorded fact, in the joins, in the DM notebook, and (ruling 27; car 18n NOT YET LANDED) in AT MOST ONE OBSERVED FACE PER VARIANT, tagged `[archiver · observed]` — the archiver as witness, a BARE OBSERVATION in the passive with no observer named (ruling 40: 'No soldier has been seen on the wall at night'; 'The stair to the walk has stores stacked on it and nobody has moved them'): a FACT the archiver claims, so floor 1 binds it hardest (never a body a required row seats denied — the card's section (2b) is the observer's list of what it may not have seen), and floor 2 with the elapsed course carried WITHOUT A NUMBER ('on any night the survey kept', never 'four days'); the selector keeps observed candidates under NOTES until the car lands. THE PUBLIC OBSERVES (ruling 28; the same car): one candidate per variant tagged `[public]` — the town's people as a whole, owed to no power, for what everyone saw at once and nobody disputes ('Everyone in the town has seen the wall kept and nobody on it'; 'It is common knowledge here that the gate is barred at dusk by whoever is nearest') — the COLLECTIVE PERCEIVED TRUTH: what everyone SAW is true (floor 1 in full, no inference, never a field's opposite) and what everyone MAKES OF IT is the town's shared perception, which may be mistaken about meaning and never about the thing seen ('…and takes it that nobody is coming'); no power's stake, no reassurance on a source's behalf, never a covert fact, plural roles only ('the townsfolk', 'anyone in the village'); kept under NOTES until the car lands. THE ARCHIVER IS UNBIASED BY POWERS AND BIASED PERSONALLY (ruling 19): the attribution is symmetric — no source gets a warmer verb or more room than another — and the archiver's TEMPERAMENT (the card prints it; default 'exact'; the set: dry · anxious · fond of the town · weary of it · exact) colours only the joins and the notebook's shades, never the facts and never which source is heard. PLAIN LANGUAGE (ruling 20): the caliber is in the design — the sources, the stakes, the pairs, the withheld reason — and the WORDS are the plainest that carry it: common verbs, concrete nouns, no archaism, no inversion, no mannered attribution ('the hall says', 'the guilds say openly', 'at the tavern they say', 'the survey finds', never 'would like it noted' or 'were public in stating'); a game master scanning at a glance reads every line once; 'the wall is kept up, nobody is paid to guard it', not 'the works are kept, nobody is paid to stand on them'. PLAIN IS NOT CASUAL: no contractions, no slang, no barroom idiom — the exact common word in a professional's hand ('the toll figures do not match… the gate takes its share, the hall records less than that', never 'the numbers don't add up… the gate takes its cut'). ARCHAIC WORDS, MIXED IN WHERE THE MEANING STAYS TRANSPARENT: plain by default, but a period word is welcome where it is the right word and the sentence carries its meaning without a glossary ('paid in kind, goods not coin'; 'the moot' after a line that showed it deciding; 'the reeve' where the sentence shows the reeve collecting) — never two in one sentence, never in the attribution verb. The archiver's own sentences are RESTRAINED: the fact, and then the stop. 'The works are kept. Nobody is paid to stand on them.' — two facts, no explanation, the reader does the arithmetic. THE TELLS THE ARCHIVER NEVER WRITES (the exemplar pack's own list; the selector's veto list, never a refuter's finding): the which-clause closer ('which tells you what it is for'), the summarising close, the antithesis pair, the reassurance, the clever last beat that explains the sentence before it. Between two lawful candidates the shorter one that stops sooner wins. The card names the speakers that exist on EVERY town this pool can draw (the hall's clerk, whoever holds the way through, the muster, the temple's registrar, the guild's factor, the crown's assessor, the tavern, the stranger, and the others the holder table seats) and the INTEREST each carries from the town's own power fields. A face reports a source as the archiver would set it down — the hall defending its purse, the muster's grievance with the hall, the tavern's account of who turns out, a stranger's report of what the town has stopped noticing. Two sources may read one fact differently; the archiver sets both down and the disagreement is the session. THE ARCHIVER MAY WEIGH IN (ruling 22): on a disagree or reinforce pair ONE further sentence in the archiver's own hand may close the unit — NOT a restatement of the survey (the spine already spoke) but the ARCHIVER'S OWN CONJECTURE, or that the matter is in dispute, or the archiver's confidence in the two ('It may be that both are right, and the one purse is filled at the gate and spent by the hall'; 'Which is right is a matter of debate in the town'; 'The hall's account is the likelier, the guilds counting every toll as a loss' — never 'the office' or 'this record' on the player page (ruling 40); on a reinforce pair 'On this the two agree, and the office sees no cause to doubt them') — a lean is licensed only with its reason drawn from the other source's stake, and no source is favoured across a block; never a covert field, never a field's opposite; THE WEIGHING OPENS AND NEVER CLOSES (the owner: the game master decides what actually happens; the dossier only offers the opportunity) — a guess is the office's guess, a dispute is left standing, a confidence is an opinion and never a verdict, and a weighing that reads as a ruling on the facts is refused; THE ONE EXCEPTION is a covert fact the engine holds (the card's hard covert state): the notebook note carries it and there the dossier does settle it for the game master; where the card holds no such fact, nothing on either page settles the question — a note or a weighing that closes what the engine left open is refused; no notebook devices on the player page; the tells bar it as any face; three sentences in the unit at most. A PAIR MAY BE ONE COMPOUND SENTENCE (ruling 23): the archiver may join the two accounts with the pair's own conjunction — disagree: 'though' · 'but' · 'while' · 'and yet'; reinforce: 'and' · 'as' — 'The hall says the wall is paid for out of the common purse, though at the tavern they say it is paid at the gate' — so the second attribution rides inside the sentence; write a pair's faces as single clauses that read after such a joint where you can, and mark the pair `joinable` in a comment; the render chooses the joint or the full stop, seeded (car 18i; until it lands the full stop); never a semicolon. No source asserts a field's opposite — EXCEPT THE COMPROMISED ROLE (ruling 26; car 18m NOT YET LANDED): where the card's covert section names a field that compromises a source (a captured hall, a watch under a covert bloc, an unexposed officer, a register under a cult), the writers offer ONE extra candidate for that source per variant, tagged `[<source> · compromised]`, in which the source conceals — dismisses, reassures, minimises, changes the subject, blames the talk — about the symptom of its own secret and nothing else, in MORE THAN ONE SHAPE across the variants so reassurance never becomes a signal; reported as flatly as any account; and one honest, TRUE reassurance per pool may be offered untagged where the field it reassures on genuinely holds, so a reassurance on the page is a question and never an answer; the selector keeps them under a separate heading in NOTES until 18m lands; the refuter judges them under the inverted test (they must reassure on their own field's symptom and deny nothing else). THE SOURCES THAT EXIST EVERYWHERE the pool can draw are the safe ones (the card lists them): a stranger always; the elders below town; at town and above the hall (say 'the hall' or 'a clerk in the hall', never 'the clerk' or 'the mayor'), the tavern, the guilds, the register (say 'the register' or 'the sexton', never 'the priest'); the muster, the watch and whoever holds the way through ONLY where the key fixes their seat; the crown's assessor NOWHERE — the engine has no typed crown collector. ATTRIBUTION BY ROLE (ruling 25 as the owner amended it; car 18l NOT YET LANDED): the tier's named OFFICES may speak as titles where the roster prints them ('the mayor', 'the parish priest', 'the guard captain'), never by the office-holder's minted name and never fixing a fate; the page will print a ROLE drawn from the town's own rows for each source ('a clerk in the hall', 'a local priest', 'dock workers', 'a traveller') in place of the class word — write the attribution so that any role of that source could stand in it ('the hall says' → a person of the hall says), keep the class word until the car lands, and never lean on the class word's plurality ('the guilds, all of them'). A SOURCE SPEAKS FROM ITS STANDING (ruling 18): where the card prints a source's rank band in this town (a faith's cult · established · ascendant; a faction's influence rank; the hall's legitimacy) the reported view is written from that standing — an ascendant register holds that the works are a want of faith and says so to the hall, a cult keeps its opinion inside its own door — and where the stake turns with rank the writer offers a face per band, tagged, so the draw never gives a fringe creed a zealot's line (the deity rows D-04–D-10 bind: the zeal is the followers', follows their standing, never the pantheon rank, never the god). AND THAT GOES FOR EVERY POWER'S ACCOUNT: the hall from its legitimacy and capture, the guilds from their influence and the town's prosperity, the watch and the muster from what the purse pays them, the tavern from the safety label and the stress at hand, the stranger from the town's posture. A SOURCE'S ACCOUNT CARRIES ITS OWN FEELING, sized to its standing: greed ('the guilds say the bar costs them more than it costs the town'), eagerness ('the muster says it would stand the wall tomorrow if the wall were paid for'), fear, pride, grievance, zeal — reported, never the archiver's, and never a warmer verb for one source than another. A source's OPINION is free; a source's MEMORY is not — a durative only over the key's own reads and the frozen fields the card marks. DM SECRETS ARE THE ARCHIVER'S MARGINAL NOTES (ruling 17): a `dm-only` face is conjecture or suspicion in the archiver's own hand — a sum that does not close, a thing seen more often than its trade explains, 'it may be that' — never a settled term; the suspicion points TOWARD the recorded covert fact and never away from it (a note that suspects the opposite of the field is a finding); the panel beside it states the fact plainly; the card says which covert fields are hard state (a note that carries them) and which are fleeting (the lightest touch); the dm-only mark itself is unchanged and no player face carries a margin note. THE NOTEBOOK REGISTER (ruling 17 amended): the DM face is the archiver's notebook where the player face is the fair copy — STILL IN THE THIRD PERSON — the notebook is a professional's working notes kept for whoever holds the office next, never a diary: no 'I' anywhere in the dossier, nothing assumed of the reader's memory, the thing it circles named so a successor picks the thread up cold — the rhythm loosens, the archiver explains their own reasoning, and the note is TRANSPARENT: it names what it circles and why, so the game master never guesses which fact the suspicion points at; floor 2 still binds (no dates, no counts — 'the figures do not close', never a sum) and the tier's named offices stay unnamed ('whoever keeps the toll book'). STILL TWO SENTENCES AT MOST: the notebook loosens the rhythm, never the length — one or two sentences per face, DM or player. THE NOTEBOOK'S DEVICES, licensed on dm-only faces only: the ellipsis '…', the colon, the aside in commas, the unfinished clause, a question left hanging without its mark, a word picked up again — never the em dash, the exclamation mark or a digit (the ratchet and floor 2 bar them everywhere). Example of the register: 'The toll figures do not close… the bar takes what it takes, the hall's return is short of it, and nobody at the hall has asked why. If the watch is paid twice, then whose is the second purse.' THE NOTEBOOK'S RANGE: the notes vary in SHADE — accusatory, conjecture, unsure, hearsay, and everything between — never one suspicion in five coats; the writer offers different shades and the selector spreads them across the block's dm-only faces; at every shade an accusation is a note, not a verdict, names no singular office, and points toward the fact. THE ARCHIVER'S OWN FEELING is licensed in the notebook — worry, hope, unease, in the third person ('It is to be hoped that the watch is not tempted to open the way at night, or to mutiny over it') — as the plainest way of saying what is at stake; THE CLARITY TEST: a game master can say in one breath what the note suspects and what could come of it, or the note fails. THE FEELING HAS A RANGE (the owner): worry, hope, unease, EAGERNESS (a thread worth following — 'if the second purse is the bar's, then the bar can be asked, and it would be worth the asking'), CLARITY (the note that settles what the fair copy could not — 'there is only one hand the figures pass through: whoever keeps the toll book'), SURPRISE ('the register is full for a place this size… fuller than the tavern's account of who turns out; one of the two is counting wrong'), CURIOSITY ('where the second purse comes from is in no account; it would be worth knowing who fills it'); the writer offers notes in different feelings and the selector spreads them across the block's dm-only faces as it spreads the shades. GREED, PRIDE, FEAR, GRIEVANCE AND ZEAL BELONG TO THE SOURCES, never to the archiver: the archiver sets a source's feeling down in attributed speech and keeps their own to the notebook. THE THREE OLD STANCES ([ledger] the office's record, [street] the town's own talk, [visitor] a stranger's eye) SURVIVE AS THE SPINE LINE'S TAG and as families of speakers, never as a voice without a person behind it. THE TOWN NEVER NAMES ITSELF INSIDE A FACE (ruling 12): {settlement} appears in at most one unit of a pool and never in a '[face]' sub-row; the town is 'the town', 'here', 'the place', or implied — the reader is standing in its dossier. THE MECHANICAL VOICE RULES that red the build and stand: no em dash, no exclamation mark, no digit; four faces per variant, each a DIFFERENT sentence (a different subject, order and landing noun, never a paraphrase of a sibling); never trim; a face must stand alone, since an unweighted seeded roll picks it at render, and read well after the spine or any sibling. WHAT IS STRUCK AND MAY NOT BE APPLIED FROM MEMORY: 'every claim licensed by the card'; 'keep every claim, add none'; the same claim set on every face (a face need not restate its key at all: twelve faces are twelve things a person could notice in a town where the key holds); the always-safe spelling lists; the layer bar; the record-word bar; the person bar beyond floor 3; the per-face taste bands."

